From 43f32725248e551c4a9e38ed34164a799e2e68c2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: qiyinxi <qiyinxi@foxmail.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 18:56:05 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 113/116] MAS local: boot at the configured orientation after
 the rotate button

The rotate button sets Android's display rotation with `settings put
system user_rotation N`, and that setting is persistent: after a shutdown
in portrait Android started the next boot at ROTATION_270 while the window
started landscape from hw.initialOrientation, and the lock screen came up
turned for some seconds, until the manager set user_rotation 0 after the
boot. An emulator started without the manager stayed turned.

- As the guest boots, the window sets user_rotation to what goes with the
  orientation it started at, as soon as Android's settings service answers
  (one adb command: wait-for-device, then `settings put` every quarter
  second for up to a minute), before the lock screen. It turns nothing: the
  window is at that orientation already. Only where the button could be
  used (the manager's adb server, an adb). A click during the boot cancels
  it.
- The window's own safe shutdown (the close button, Windows ending the
  session) puts user_rotation back to the start's first, in the same guest
  shell as its first sync (SafeShutdown::Actions::beforeSync), when the
  button left it otherwise.
- The button turns between the orientation the window started at
  (hw.initialOrientation; the manager is about to allow portrait) and the
  other one, rather than always landscape and a quarter turn clockwise, and
  reads the window's rotation rather than the sensors'.

Test: aemu-ui-lean-window_unittests (57), aemu-ui-safe-shutdown_unittests
(17). Mutation check of the new logic: 5 of 5 killed.

Refs ModAVD/ModAVD#225

Change-Id: Ifa05e64f1fc766cd87bb9179cfe9a330ae91fb88
